// Static type checking for the Python layers. Gating in lint.yml.
//
// mode: `recommended`. It was `standard` while the alternative looked like 126
//   errors; measured on the current tree it is 470 root annotations across the
//   source, and that number is the whole reason this is worth doing. The
//   Unknown* family — 2,209 diagnostics — is not independent work, it is the
//   shadow of those 470. ownership.py was the proof: 29 diagnostics from four
//   bare `dict` annotations in signatures, all 29 gone once the four were typed.
//   Leverage across the source files ran 3x to 16x.
//
//   What that buys, concretely: the JSON shapes this repo passes between modules
//   (FixtureResult, the summary rows, the comparison, the snapshots) are now
//   types rather than `dict`, so a renamed key fails here instead of as a
//   KeyError mid-run in the heavy eval workflow.
//
// Warnings are not tolerated either. basedpyright exits non-zero only on errors,
//   so `recommended` would technically pass with thousands of warnings standing —
//   and a checker printing 7,000 lines is one nobody reads. Clean means clean.
//
// Three of `recommended`'s rules are off, and only these three. Each costs real
// edits and makes the code worse, which is a different thing from being hard:
//
//   reportUnusedCallResult — wants `_ = f()` for every discarded return. That is
//     128 sites, and it would put `_ =` in front of every `lines.append` and
//     `print` in the repo. Not Python idiom, and it obscures the lines it marks.
//   reportImplicitStringConcatenation — flags adjacent string literals across
//     lines, which is exactly the `para(...)` prose style eval/summary.py uses
//     deliberately so that hard-wrapped source does not leak line breaks into a
//     GitHub step summary. 41 sites, all of them the intended style.
//   reportPrivateUsage / reportPrivateLocalImportUsage — 143 sites, nearly all
//     tests reaching for a `_`-prefixed helper (E._first_sales_channel_id,
//     S._usd, R._payload). There is a real argument that a helper a test needs is
//     part of the contract and should be public; that rename is a follow-up, not
//     a reason to hold up the rest.
//
// pythonVersion: matches .python-version so stdlib and syntax are checked
//   against the interpreter CI actually runs.
//
// scope: source for now; tests/ lands next, in its own commit. It is 850 more
//   root annotations and two structural changes — typed factories for the fakes,
//   and marking FixtureResult's always-present fields Required — which is a
//   different review from this one. `exclude` AND `ignore` both name it until
//   then: exclude keeps the CI run out, but an editor still checks any file you
//   OPEN even when excluded, and `ignore` is what makes the two agree.
{
  "typeCheckingMode": "recommended",
  "pythonVersion": "3.14",
  "reportUnusedCallResult": "none",
  "reportImplicitStringConcatenation": "none",
  "reportPrivateUsage": "none",
  "reportPrivateLocalImportUsage": "none",
  "include": [
    "eval",
    "functional",
    "lane.py",
    "mcp_client.py",
    "ownership.py",
    "toolclass.py",
    "ucp.py",
    "toollint.py"
  ],
  "exclude": [
    "**/__pycache__",
    "**/node_modules",
    ".venv",
    "tests"
  ],
  "ignore": [
    "tests"
  ]
}
